#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175761 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "e1c44d04-1798-5619-813d-a75e8f05140d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9087" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/1a6c3918b93419f9576951374ebd69b2df271106345493084ffa0797b0a35847.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-3$ and $\\lambda_2=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/d6c87739f52f450b27c68a118bba04e51e68a377da7d0a16ae0658e1b2d547f0.png)\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/7bd509d943bf0707e75c9436aee3a511f8366502bbf8121b715cec8ce366953b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "d60be4d7-335c-564a-bc75-52a2f76d660f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9225" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/69fcceae9cb181cf79e1b12f0db37ed2ba27841aaebb089ffa24d0d365b9db56.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/c8716d15efb054f488535b139e201dda7bc61a3fb8880caf50d65bee515c2cc1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "511398e8-780e-57b2-8f1b-092e9f6cca45" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Scalar Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "16a3033b-9110-5e1f-914d-8351bdc5f930" :question/math-academy-id "e-9226" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/4b66afe6c1771afad4987215e3ddc02865a9f873ae93050f940c36bc3ff86356.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda = -3,$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/2be33154f6a1933a12a3c43474464edb9c4e3fd2b2a4213d789b49fc1e4aec77.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b17622-2bed-5fdf-a001-6fed60a0e93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173545} {:answer/id #uuid "c4667d2e-b3e6-57a3-af29-52f2fb24c0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173546} {:answer/id #uuid "fd725588-9adf-5c5c-949f-ec236a9b9a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173547} {:answer/id #uuid "3d63b09a-3934-5fe3-bcc2-33aac9c09e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173548} {:answer/id #uuid "3e409f37-33b3-5d46-ac67-07b2990b8f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4667d2e-b3e6-57a3-af29-52f2fb24c0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173546} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1148468-12b7-54d1-9cd0-e88e06b85167" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a50741c-c4f5-5a02-bb86-14c9da06d5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-108719" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9be8aa47-6155-54c6-9a7d-53eeda148e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368080} {:answer/id #uuid "d47c5843-17c5-532e-8d63-344c6b076de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368081} {:answer/id #uuid "db6f153c-5e38-5f8d-bd42-8938b36f96d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368082} {:answer/id #uuid "83e96c1a-ebae-5656-a09b-02462f524c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368083} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdfb274-f496-5caa-b48c-e5a39018b71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6f153c-5e38-5f8d-bd42-8938b36f96d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368082} :answer-field/id #uuid "241546c4-0f61-5343-bfcf-78835c112df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c5c13a5-e730-55ff-8103-21c10930b912" :question/math-academy-id "q-109095" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef5e1b6d-4762-5b47-b542-3d12533a35e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368087} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d4bf1a-85a9-55c5-9b9b-76a2d8a795ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368088} {:answer/id #uuid "33e99932-83ac-55db-a6af-a07559c10d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368089} {:answer/id #uuid "43078ef0-fb93-515e-88e6-190390317c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368090} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f298e0-685c-5fbe-8333-ab2258a71285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f298e0-685c-5fbe-8333-ab2258a71285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8001822-acad-5ff5-bd48-868ceac85844" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07aab14d-6173-554d-a730-1e67dec81067" :question/math-academy-id "q-109102" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e264fa1-db8c-526d-ab4d-9e1cbd9f16fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368094} {:answer/id #uuid "90358b8e-794b-5975-a10f-dd063b6ac749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368095} {:answer/id #uuid "618e6740-51f3-5b1f-b5ce-f6dc9d8fa516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368096} {:answer/id #uuid "823e22ab-a812-5619-8226-046e482958cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368097} {:answer/id #uuid "ae58d280-4efa-5990-940a-8bb40ba94204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae58d280-4efa-5990-940a-8bb40ba94204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368098} :answer-field/id #uuid "54fca6dc-ae55-5cb2-87d8-c3b7056c680f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc5a7df0-4070-58fb-ae5f-1f54dcbfbce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-109315" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid 