n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(2)(2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(-3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6560770a-cb46-515c-81ef-c95389592e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368108} {:answer/id #uuid "c4aacab1-bb98-5d84-b8f7-c087835f59cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368109} {:answer/id #uuid "0f595ddf-9cdd-57d5-917f-d3af0b82e9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368110} {:answer/id #uuid "3a32b6af-7b15-5a1e-83f6-54df1a01ad10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368111} {:answer/id #uuid "77aa115b-3acb-5219-8da6-b669ccea4b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77aa115b-3acb-5219-8da6-b669ccea4b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218