wo distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(6)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81b22c5-7e32-50d0-98b3-e7e484d05554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368156} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc91a10-d76e-54c9-a9bc-b90eeb531f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368157} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba0b44b-6a53-58ed-88f8-f091f6846771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368158} {:answer/id #uuid "d14b2195-e846-5e33-a2db-01f5d928dd38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368159} {:answer/id #uuid "d659f57b-1912-59fe-804a-7d0e18832370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d81b22c5-7e32-50d0-98b3-e7e484d05554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368156} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f87780-fda4-59f5-b739-abd8f730f672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e683ad88-dd0a-5bae-b8ce-ef0171100317" :question/math-academy-id "q-112460" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-(2)(-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9198e673-7cd0-53e1-b79a-54f68a98d4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368163} {:answer/id #uuid "8da52ed9-e674-5a1d-a1ea-3f54e91822b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368164} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8199fe-cfa2-5a64-a0e8-32ee8e994e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368165} {:answer/id #uuid "e4665c0b-c5e3-542f-8d6d-3d4b7265314c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368166} {:answer/id #uuid "88dd73ac-cc0c-5a2d-954b-cba7393aba77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4665c0b-c5e3-542f-8d6d-3d4b7265314c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368166} :answer-field/id #uuid "44197e59-b44b-5e08-a254-1f9dc3154d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a1f67ef-dd2a-5d98-a6ec-dc6e37a110d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-112477" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-12-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 7 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-12-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-7)\\cdot 7 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-5)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c444a668-0749-59fa-8660-27b6ce1f9df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368170} {:answer/id #uuid "76a77edc-e941-5fac-9064-ec7deed147de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368171} {:answer/id #uuid "17c86621-9a4e-5c99-892a-b8ce2d3b1e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368172} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1705fc-2149-5206-af71-0a54dabaf4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368173} {:answer/id #uuid "b26a274b-2cac-52c0-9675-0763b86770e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c86621-9a4e-5c99-892a-b8ce2d3b1e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368172} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6d9841-8e9b-5aa1-9f8d-f6bcdf775f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58112fb5-e289-5b2d-b171-03f84610dc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-112478" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(9)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
